Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"Aristotelian merit is virtue rather than endowment, and so escapes the natural contingency objection":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the fact that Aristotle’s conception of virtue is something that is cultivated rather than endowed, it would escape the natural contingency objection.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that Aristotle’s view of virtue is not grounded in the initial nature into which one is born.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that we must repeatedly practice virtue in order to become virtuous, and that no one is born naturally virtuous. However, this he does qualify this by saying that natural virtue exists, which itself is not virtue, but merely a greater tendency for one to pursue. So really, Aristotle’s position in complex: while the process of attaining virtue itself is something that the individual must work for, some individuals are naturally better disposed to attaining virtue than others.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Fundamentally, however, it seems that if all of us have different levels of natural virtue, and this is what determine whether we will be able to achieve virtue later in life, then it doesn’t seem that we are able to escape the natural contingency argument. It seems still predetermined.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a><ul><li>Aristotle would probably agree, since he believe that justice involved distributing goods according to one’s relative merit in the world.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a><ul><li>Aristotle would disagree with the idea that natural contingencies would be shared between members of a society, as per the previous comment.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This is Aristotle’s view regarding attaining virtue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself\">Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself</a><ul><li>His view towards what are inherently born with.</li></ul></li></ul>","Christ Embracing Saint Bernard":"<h4>Addendum</h4><p>Perhaps the best depictions of Christ and the cross? The figures are highly expressive and the composition only highlights the feeling. Christ’s movement down the cross is dignified and divine but humble and humanistic at the same time. Saint Bernard is perhaps representative of humanity as a collective, embracing Christ.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christ on the Cross\">Christ on the Cross</a><ul><li>Another image of Chris and the Cross, though this one is much more mystical and divine.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Descent from the Cross\">The Descent from the Cross</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is a good contrast as being the opposite of the El Greco linked above, as this is perhaps the most human portray of Christ and the cross.</li></ul></li></ul>","Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Formal equality of opportunity requires that institutions apply their rules impartially to everyone, while substantive equality of opportunity requires that people arrive at the competition with comparable resources, and a meritocracy is a society in which both obtain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Almost every defence of meritocracy in public argument establishes only the formal sense, which is the weaker of the two and does no work against inherited advantage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a><ul><li>The Rawlsian version of the substantive requirement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>Why the formal sense alone is insufficient.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>The standing obstacle to the substantive sense.</li></ul></li></ul>","Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A city should be composed “as far as possible, of equals and similars.” The very rich cannot obey and the very poor cannot command, so a city split between them is one of masters and slaves rather than of citizens, whatever its laws say.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Explains why extending the franchise is insufficient, and why the distribution of condition is a constitutional question.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The argument runs through character, not resources directly. Those with excessive goods of fortune are neither willing nor able to submit to authority, never having learned obedience; the very poor are too degraded to do anything but obey. Neither extreme readily follows reason, and the resulting contempt and envy destroy the <em>philia</em> a city depends on. Democracy has legal equality by definition, since it reckons equality by number. But that secures nothing about condition, and both extreme democracy and pure oligarchy arise “where some possess much, and the others nothing.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office\">Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office</a><ul><li>Supplies the formal entitlement this note argues is insufficient alone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society\">Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society</a><ul><li>The constructive half of the same argument.</li></ul></li></ul>","Goodwill is not friendship":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle distinguishes between “goodwill” and “friendship,” where the former occurs when we feel some merit and good towards someone, but the other person is not aware of these feelings and these feelings are largely superficial.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is important to distinguish between friendship and good will since many of the things that apply to friendship do not apply to goodwill.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a><ul><li>The contents of this note describe something that is different from the content of this link.</li></ul></li></ul>","In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a conductor, because the charged particles want to stay away from each other, they move to the edge of the conductor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that on the inside, a conductor is in electrostatic equilibrium.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Free electron movement is the precondition for charge reaching the surface.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds\">Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds</a><ul><li>Explains why whole-body redistribution is possible in a conductor but not an insulator.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Identifies Fishkin’s trilemma, a fundamental problem with liberal societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This work was an integral part of the debate over meritocracy and the possibilities for the existence of a truly meritocratic society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Three core tenants of liberal thought cannot be maintained together simultaneously:</p><ol><li><strong>The Principle of Merit (M):</strong> That positions in society should be allocated based on relevant qualifications, disregarding irrelevant characteristics.<ol><li><em>This draws on the concept of procedural justice, which is most well explicated as the concept of pure procedural justice by Rawls. Fishkin directly draws on [@barryPoliticalArgumentReissue1990, p. 97-98]</em></li></ol></li><li><strong>Equality of Life Chances (ELC):</strong> That one’s position in society is not determined by the circumstances of their birth.<ol><li>See <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy › ^precise-ELC</a></li></ol></li><li><strong>Autonomy of the Family (FA):</strong> That parent’s are permitted to substantially influence the development of their children.<ol><li>So this basically just comes down to a matter of parenting style.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Now, any combination of two of these principles is crucial for a liberal society in any conventional sense of the word. Essentially, the idea that any realistic large-scale modern society will have substantial social and economic inequalities. Yet, when any two are indeed synthesizes, the result is that the third principle is excluded:</p><ul><li>M + ELC entails that children must develop under equal conditions, which requires overriding FA.<ul><li><code>any empirical studies about how/whether there could be the possibility of having some kind of a problem where the state ensures equality through developmental programs that are compatible with FA?</code></li></ul></li><li>M + FA entails that children from more well-off families will systematically develop superior qualifications, violating ELC.<ul><li>This is documented in [@markovitsMeritocracyTrapHow2019].</li></ul></li><li><em>ELC + FA is a unique case, because it is self-defeating without even needing to invoke M. ELC states that all aspects of children that are arbitrary ought to be equalized, yet that would mean equalizing the family situation which undermines FA.</em><ul><li><em>In other words, such a society simply cannot exist, let alone be liberal.</em></li><li>I have empirical evidence that differences in family circumstance leads to differences in outcome of the child.</li><li>Even if this is not the case, ELC + FA and assignment of employment according to some lottery would undermine liberty of employment.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>Underscores FA.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s contribution to the idea of the importance of family, related to FA.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>A general coverage of the concept of meritocracy, related to M from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under Rawls’ framework, the only legitimate reason to restrict the rights of some is in order to protect the overall system of equal basic liberties or the strengthen it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important principles that Rawls establishes, since it ensures that liberty is the basic principle that no other principle may disturb.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are roughly two ways in order liberty may be diminished:</p><ol><li>Liberty can overall be less extensive, but still maintaining equal liberty. This requires that some specific liberty be narrowed for everyone equally, leading to a net gain in liberty for the representative individual.</li><li>Liberty can be made unequal. This is canonically with the case of children, since they loss of liberty enhances their freedom and allows them to develop properly.</li></ol><p>There are also two ways in which restrictions in liberty may arise.</p><ul><li>Natural limitations: there is something about the nature of the society/the place that the society inhabits that necessitates limitations on the liberty of some or the whole.</li><li>Existing injustice: this is the case whereby existing social arrangements already violate the principles of justice. This concerns the domain of non-ideal theory.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a><ul><li>The intolerant could have their rights reduced if they go too far.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental principle that this note is trying to establish.</li></ul></li></ul>","Man is created in the image of God":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The theistic idea that humans were created in the image of God.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has many ethical and theological implications, including for morality.</p><h4>Bible</h4><ul><li>Adam goes about and names the animals, something that God ought to do. This symbolizes how humans have dominion over the Earth.</li><li>Humans have a special moral status, since they are in the image of God, and thus murder is prohibited.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ethics is grounded in religion\">Ethics is grounded in religion</a><ul><li>The current note would support this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Marx claims to lead a new, secular revolution, the underlying structure of his claims and the meaning of his action is able to be precisely modeled upon a Christian perspective.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Lowith’s analysis of Marx’s historical viewpoints.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many similarities between Marx’s view of history and the Christian eschatological view of history.</p><ul><li>Marx sees the proletariat as the world-historical instrument that exists outside existing society and serves as the driving force that actualizes the world’s potential for a classless, absolute society.<ul><li>It is only with a eschatological view of history that Marx is able to state that the proletariat is the “heart” of history, while his ideas are the “brains” of history.</li></ul></li><li>Marx’s view of exploitation is the supreme moral fact that the world revolution is justified upon.<ul><li>It is merely an ethical judgement, and is not empirical, as Marx claims that his theory overall is. If the very fundamental nature of a theory is merely ethical, then it hardly seems plausible to call the overall theory “empirical.”</li><li>“Exploitation” can be equated to the “original sin” in Christian theology.</li></ul></li><li>The classless society prophesized by the <em>Communist Manifesto</em> is the Kingdom of God on Earth.</li><li>Marx generalizes his critique to being one that is against all “idols,” not just Christianity specifically, but also capitalism. A fight against earthly idols is not what scientists do; it’s what prophets do.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 2, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 42</p><p>[A] Kingdom of God, without God and on earth, […] is the ultimate goal and ideal of Marx's historical messianism. […] <strong>Though perverted into secular prognostication, the Communist Manifesto still retains the basic features of a messianic faith: the assurance of things to be hoped for.</strong> […] It would have been quite impossible to elaborate the vision of the proletariat's messianic vocation on a purely scientific basis and to inspire millions of followers by a bare statement of facts.</p></blockquote><p>Marx critiques religion by simply dismissing it, thinking that the work has already been done by other scholars. He fails, however, to analyze the reason for the existence of religion in the first place. And by destroying religion, Marx is essentially supplying a substitute in the form of Marxism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos\">People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos</a><ul><li>This is what is said in the quote above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marx is more idealistic than Hegel\">Marx is more idealistic than Hegel</a><ul><li>This can be seen through Marx’s critique of religion, which says essentially that humanity needs to entirely override our existing conceptions of religion and… replace it with a whole other “correct” religion.</li></ul></li></ul>","Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Once the Zen sage has achieved enlightenment, they return to living their normal lives, though they have been changed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what the Zen doctrine instructs its students to do.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Once one has achieved enlightenment, they return to living their lives, such that while their actions remain the same, they are no longer the same person. With the achievement of enlightenment, the sage loses their attachment to everything.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a><ul><li>This is the process by which one achieves enlightenment.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our best friend is ourselves":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One’s best friend is themselves, meaning that one loves oneself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle’s idea of self-love is an important aspect of his philosophy that is rather rare.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>A virtuous man is able to be virtuous to others, so he must be able to be virtuous to himself.</li><li>Importantly, the “self-love” described here is not referring to a type of immoral self-love whereby we attempt to secure for ourselves the maximum amount of money, or honor, or bodily pleasure. <ul><li>This is referring acting virtuously towards oneself.</li><li>This also means that a bad man cannot have self-love in the true sense, and the bad man hates himself.</li></ul></li><li>Since we are our own best friend, it means that we also love ourselves (virtuously) most.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Descartes’ famous saying of <em>cognito ergo sum</em> (\"I think, therefore I am\") seem to be at the end of an intense and highly skeptical process of deconstructing the accepted epistemology.  However, at the end of this process, Descartes constructs a new theory upon the old one, this time, based on the divine.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This process of first deconstructing our epistemology, and then reconstructing it upon the divine is the core project of Descartes’ <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reductivism regarding person identity means that the self is merely a bundle of experience, accumulated over time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This view of personal identity improves upon the egoist theory of the self in Parfit‘s view.</p><h4>Reductivism</h4><p>This is Derek Parfit‘s view of personal identity.</p><ul><li>In this view, the self is a bundle of experiences, where the self is determined by psychological connectedness.<ul><li>Psychological connection is continuity of experience. This psychological connection is the causal connection is the glue that binds everything together? <ul><li>Ex. The sight of a chair causes a belief in the existence of the chair.</li></ul></li><li>Our life is a series of bundles of mental states at any moment (your beliefs, our desires, your thoughts, etc.)</li></ul></li></ul><p>Rather than claiming the existence of an egoist self, Parfit argues that the self is defined by the accumulation of experience in an individual over time.</p><ul><li>The ego view states that there is a special \"me-ness\" to someone.</li><li>Time is not the best proxy for this, since there could be really radical change in a very short period of time.</li></ul><p>Parfit argues that personal identity is not what we should care about from a self-interested perspective. All that matters is continuity of existence.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Psychological continuity exists on a continuum\">Psychological continuity exists on a continuum</a><ul><li>Parfit elaborates this thing that we argues we should actually care about.</li></ul></li></ul>","Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Selective incentives are benefits given out on an individual/household level as to incentivize people to contribute to some cause, or as to discourage people from not participating.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the existence and strength of many different political interest groups. Additionally, it solves the free-rider problem in large groups.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Labor unions: While all workers benefit from the wage negotiations carried out by labor unions, those who join the union get additional benefits, such as union-only legal support or strike pay.</li><li>Professional associations: While such organizations create public good as they enforce stronger profession-wide regulations, members are the only ones that are able to benefit from their networking events and get access to the services that the association offers at a discounted rate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Shock therapy failed in Russia due to lack of functioning market institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Shock therapy failed in Russia due the lack of well-functioning market institutions that would have allowed the transition from state-run economy to capitalism smoothly. Rather, it lead to asset stripping by the Russian elite.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the failure of Russian shock therapy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good contrastive example of “shock therapy” working is the privatization of the zaibatsus during the Meiji era. Similar sales were made by the government to an elite selection of families in the public. However, the prices were not artificially deflated in the sale (which in Russia, led to asset stripping instead of reinvestment) and there were well-functioning state institutions in order to carefully enforce property rights and ensure that the whole privatization process was carried out successfully.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>This note is some evidence against this idea, since it is often the result of weak state institutions, and not capitalism itself, which is the cause for the negative effects.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Directly supported by the current note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the Absurd man, since traditional values and meanings are no longer considered, the only value in the world exists in the form of aesthetic/sensory value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the nature of the Absurd man in the face of the lack of moral values and guidance.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the Absurd man, life is simply a series of impressions. There is no value attached to the meanings constructed above this layer of sensory experience. Where a normal person could claim to “love” even when they are not thinking about the object of their “love,” and they would even be willing to suffer physically/mentally for such an abstract concept, the Absurd man finds this absurd.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">An Explication on the Stranger</p><p><strong>Since God does not exist and man dies, everything is permissible. One experience is as good as another; the important thing is simply to acquire as many as possible.</strong> “The ideal of the absurd man is the present and the succession of present moments before an ever-conscious spirit” (Sisyphus). Confronted with this “quantitative ethic” all values collapse; thrown into this world, the absurd man, rebellious and irresponsible, has “nothing to justify.” He is innocent, innocent as Somerset Maugham’s savages before the arrival of clergyman who teaches them Good and Evil, what is lawful and what is forbidden. For this man, everything is lawful. He is as innocent as Prince Mishkin, who “lives in an everlasting present, lightly tinged with smiles and indifference.” Innocent in every sense of the word, he, too, is, if you like, an “Idiot.”</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a><ul><li>To accept it means to seek things other than moral value. Thus, one turns to aesthetic/sensory value.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Third Man":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Holly Martins, an American writer who arrives in war-torn, divided Vienna after being invited by his old friend Harry Lime—only to discover that Lime has supposedly died in a traffic accident. Suspicious of contradictory accounts surrounding the death, Holly investigates and becomes entangled with Lime’s lover, Anna, and British officer Major Calloway. His search eventually reveals that Lime is alive and involved in a ruthless black-market scheme selling diluted penicillin, forcing Holly to confront the moral corruption of someone he once admired. Set amid Vienna’s ruins and shadowy sewers, the film explores friendship, betrayal, love, and the uneasy boundary between innocence and complicity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This film explores themes of existentialism and tragedy in an entertaining and profound manner.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>While <em>The Third Man</em> is not exactly a tragedy, it could be argued that in his pursuit of Harry’s killers, Holly alienated himself from his love interest, Anna.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>Finding out about the truth regarding the condition of his friend Harry meant that Holly now had to consider a dilemma between his loyalty to his friend and his moral duty.</li><li>For Anna, the truth does not matter. She is unceasingly loyal, and thus, the dilemma does not occur. Holly’s pursuit of the truth, in fact, is what drives her  away from him, as Anna views Holly as disloyal and a traitor.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects\">A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects</a><ul><li>This is perhaps both true for Holly and Anna?<ul><li>Holly’s determination to find out about Harry and turn him in is what prevents him from getting together with Anna.</li><li>Anna’s obstinate desire for Harry obscures the morally ignoble things that Harry has actually done.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><ul><li><strong>Harry</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, but he is willing to sacrifice others in order to save himself.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Holly</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, and he is willing to sacrifice a lower ethics (betraying his friend) in order to achieve a higher ethics (catching a murderer). </li><li>He is dangerous because he is so innocent, he cannot understand the motives of all of the evil people out there.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Anna</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, and is unchangeable in her love. </li><li>She does not change her love for Harry even after she learns that he was a murderer. </li><li>She is not ethical. She is viewing things through the individual perspective, not the universal perspective. </li><li>Her principle of non-betrayal is beyond the ethical. It is a categorical rule outside of the ethics.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Calloway</strong><ul><li>Cannot understand anything outside the ethical either. He trumps unconditional commitments with his professional work. </li><li>He is not callous; he is professional. </li><li>He is sober and professional, where as Holly is cowboy and professional.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>This work explores similar themes with regard to tragedy and truth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The irrational part of the soul that Aristotle defines is composed of the vegetative (which humans share with animals) and the appetitive.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This part of the soul is the domain of the moral virtues.</p><h4>Vegetative vs. Appetitive</h4><ul><li><strong>Vegetative:</strong> controls the basic/unconscious elements of an individual, i.e., biological growth, nutrition, sleep, etc. It functions even when the human is unconscious. Does not play a part in human excellence.</li><li><strong>Appetitive:</strong> controls the emotions, desires, and appetites of the individual. It is irrational, but is able to be tamed with reason. Moral training aligns this part of the soul with the rational part, producing the moral virtues.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative\">The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative</a><ul><li>The complementary part of the soul.</li></ul></li></ul>","The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The late 20th century saw an uptick in the amount of religious belief and the strength of such belief around the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant to Huntington’s thesis that conflict will more and more be driven by differences in religion/culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The increase in religiosity around the world is the result of urbanization/modernization making previously established belief systems inadequate and at the same time, people are not willing to accept moral relativism. Additionally, the end of competing ideologies at the end of the Cold War likewise left people directionless.</p><h4>References</h4>"});
